{"name":"Windflower","hex":"#bc9ca2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"windflower","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of wood anemone (Anemone nemorosa)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/windflower"}],"entries":[{"title":"Anemone","blocks":[{"text":"Anemone is a genus of flowering plants in the buttercup family Ranunculaceae. Plants of the genus are commonly called windflowers. They are native to the temperate and subtropical regions of all regions except Australia, New Zealand, and Antarctica. The genus is closely related to several other genera including Anemonoides, Anemonastrum, Hepatica, and Pulsatilla. Some botanists include these genera within Anemone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Anemones5.jpg/330px-Anemones5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anemone"},{"title":"Anemonoides nemorosa","blocks":[{"text":"Anemonoides nemorosa, the wood anemone, also historically known as windflower because of William Turner's interpretation of its Latin name anemone in his 1551 New Herball, is an early spring flowering plant in the buttercup family Ranunculaceae, native to Europe. It is a perennial herbaceous plant growing 5–15 cm (2–6 in) tall."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Anemone_nemorosa_001.JPG/330px-Anemone_nemorosa_001.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anemonoides_nemorosa"},{"title":"Windflower (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Windflower is an album by jazz guitarists Herb Ellis and Remo Palmier, released in 1977."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1f/Windflower_%28album%29.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windflower_(album)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedi